1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,410
Say you tap a credit card for a 10 million dong dinner and get 100,000 dong back in cashback.

2
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:06,200
It feels like free money from your bank.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,850
So who actually paid for that reward?

4
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:13,620
At the exact second the terminal beeps on your 10 million bill, zero cash leaves any account.

5
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:19,170
Your issuing bank simply puts a hold on 10 million of your credit line, waiting to advance real money later.

6
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:27,260
Two business days later in this example, the restaurant receives only 9,780,000 dong in its bank account.

7
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:32,330
The missing 220,000 dong went straight into a 2.2% card acceptance fee.

8
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,110
Where did that 220,000 dong go?

9
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:46,050
Around 150,000 dong flows directly back to your issuing bank as interchange, while the restaurant's bank keeps 60,000 and the card network takes the remaining 10,000.

10
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:59,210
Weeks later, you pay off your 10 million statement, and your bank hands you 100,000 dong in cashback — funded straight out of that 150,000 interchange cut taken from the restaurant's payout.

11
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:09,490
Because most restaurants bake card acceptance fees straight into dish prices on the menu, that shiny cashback reward may simply be your own money finishing a lap.
